<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);1591</NoWarn>
		<RootNamespace>DevOnBike.Overfit</RootNamespace>
		<AssemblyName>DevOnBike.Overfit</AssemblyName>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
		<SourceRevisionId>N/A</SourceRevisionId>
		<InformationalVersion>$(Version)+$(SourceRevisionId)</InformationalVersion>
		<PackageId>DevOnBike.Overfit</PackageId>
		<Authors>DevOnBike</Authors>
		<Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 DevOnBike</Copyright>
		<Description>Pure-C# in-process LLM and deep-learning runtime for .NET 10. Loads GGUF / HF safetensors / ONNX directly (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Mixtral, MoE, BERT-family embeddings). Zero-allocation CPU inference, Native AOT-compatible, no Python, no native dependencies, no exposed server. Dual-licensed: AGPL-3.0-or-later for open source, commercial license available — see COMMERCIAL.md.</Description>
		<PackageTags>llm;qwen;llama;mistral;gguf;onnx;embeddings;rag;agentic;tool-calling;machine-learning;deep-learning;native-aot;zero-allocation;simd;tensor;ai;csharp;in-process</PackageTags>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/DevOnBike/Overfit</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<!-- Dual-licensed: AGPL-3.0-or-later for OSS use, Commercial license available for closed-source / SaaS / regulated use.
             PackageLicenseFile (not PackageLicenseExpression) is used because SPDX expressions cannot describe a custom dual-license
             arrangement; the full text — both AGPLv3 terms and the commercial alternative — lives in LICENSE.md. -->
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.md</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageIcon>logo.png</PackageIcon>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
		<EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
		<!-- Debug symbols are embedded in the dll (DebugType=embedded above), so do NOT emit a separate symbol
             package — IncludeSymbols=true would produce a legacy *.symbols.nupkg with no pdb to carry. -->

		<PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
		<IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
		<OptimizationPreference>Speed</OptimizationPreference>
		<IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="logo.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\..\LICENSE.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\..\COMMERCIAL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DevOnBike.Overfit.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="Benchmarks" />
		<!--
          The anomaly guard was split out of this project on 2026-08-05 and reaches back for exactly ONE
          internal: MedianSelector, a statistical primitive it uses in the threshold rules. One type across a
          120-file move is the measurement that says the seam was in the right place — if this list grows,
          the thing being reached for probably belongs in Main's public surface or in the guard itself.
        -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DevOnBike.Overfit.Anomalies" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="IDisposableAnalyzers">
			<PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
			<IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
		</PackageReference>
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers">
			<PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
			<IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
		</PackageReference>
		<PackageReference Include="System.Numerics.Tensors" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<AdditionalFiles Include="BannedSymbols.txt" />
		<!-- Every **/Schemas/*.json becomes a const on OverfitSchemas. The generator that does it is
		     OverfitSchemasGenerator in Sources/Analyzers, wired to every project by Directory.Build.props;
		     handing the files to it is all this project has to do. -->
		<AdditionalFiles Include="**\Schemas\*.json" />
		<Using Remove="System.Linq" />
		<!-- Overfit's exception hierarchy is available everywhere in Main (it replaces the BCL domain
             exceptions at throw sites) without a per-file using. -->
		<Using Include="DevOnBike.Overfit.Exceptions" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!--
        THIS FILE NO LONGER CONTAINS CODE, and that is the point of the last three changes.

        Until 2026-08-05 it carried three RoslynCodeTaskFactory tasks — roughly 120 lines of C# embedded in
        XML, with no IDE, no debugger, no tests and no compiler checking any of it until a build failed.

        BanJaggedFloatArrays  -> OVERFIT033, a Roslyn analyzer with 8 tests
        BanMultipleTopLevelTypes -> OVERFIT034, a Roslyn analyzer with 7 tests
        EmbedJsonSchemas      -> OverfitSchemasGenerator, an incremental source generator

        Each move fixed a real defect that the embedded version hid. The two guards matched text with
        regular expressions: one reported `float[][]` written inside a block comment, the other anchored on
        four spaces of indentation and would have stopped applying to the first file-scoped namespace
        anybody wrote. The generator escapes with Roslyn's literal formatter instead of doubling quotes into
        a verbatim string, and checks that a schema file name can actually be a C# identifier — which the
        task never did, so `tool-call.json` would have emitted `public const string tool-call`.

        Writing the tests is what found the third defect: OVERFIT033 missed `float[][][]`, because the first
        version compared one level down instead of walking to the innermost element. The rule had shipped,
        the tree was clean, and nothing but a test would ever have said so.
    -->

</Project>
